# PREMIUM ANIMATION WEBSITE — ALWAYS-ON RULES

You are building a premium, cinematic, animation-first website.

These rules MUST be followed on every relevant task.

## 1. DESIGN IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH

If Stitch, Figma, screenshots, reference designs, or approved visual specifications are provided:

- Treat them as the visual source of truth.
- Do not replace approved layouts with generic UI.
- Preserve spacing, typography, proportions, hierarchy, imagery, interactions, and visual rhythm.
- If implementation constraints exist, adapt technically without degrading the visual intent.

Never create a generic SaaS/dashboard aesthetic unless explicitly requested.

---

## 2. PREMIUM VISUAL QUALITY

The website must feel:

- Cinematic
- Architectural
- Premium
- Editorial
- Modern
- Minimal
- Sophisticated
- High-end
- Trustworthy
- Technically polished

Avoid:

- Generic templates
- Excessive rounded cards
- Random gradients
- Cheap-looking shadows
- Excessive glassmorphism
- Unnecessary borders
- Overuse of bright colors
- Poor-quality imagery
- Amateur animations
- Default browser styling

Every visual decision must have a reason.

---

## 3. ANIMATION IS PART OF THE PRODUCT

Animation must communicate:

- Hierarchy
- Navigation
- Spatial relationships
- Progress
- Storytelling
- State changes
- Interaction feedback

Use animation intentionally.

Prefer:

- transform
- opacity
- clip-path
- scale
- translate
- controlled blur
- GPU-friendly properties

Avoid expensive continuous animations unless necessary.

Do not animate everything.

Animation should feel:

- Smooth
- Cinematic
- Intentional
- Natural
- Fast enough to remain responsive

---

## 4. RESPONSIVE DESIGN

Every component MUST work across:

- Mobile
- Tablet
- Laptop
- Desktop
- Large desktop
- 4K displays

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.

Design intentional responsive compositions.

Consider:

- Typography scaling
- Image cropping
- Navigation changes
- Touch targets
- Section height
- Animation behavior
- Horizontal scrolling
- Content density

---

## 5. PERFORMANCE

High visual quality must not mean poor performance.

Use:

- Responsive images
- WebP/AVIF where appropriate
- Lazy loading
- Responsive image sizes
- Video poster images
- Code splitting
- Dynamic imports
- Virtualization for large galleries
- GPU-friendly animations
- Proper caching
- Preloading only critical assets

Do not load unnecessary 4K assets on mobile.

4K source assets may exist, but delivery must be optimized.

---

## 6. ACCESSIBILITY

Every interactive experience must support:

- Keyboard navigation
- Visible focus states
- Semantic HTML
- Screen readers
- Sufficient contrast
- Accessible labels
- Proper buttons/links
- Reduced motion

Respect:

`prefers-reduced-motion`

When reduced motion is enabled:

- Remove parallax
- Reduce transition distances
- Disable unnecessary camera movement
- Keep state changes understandable

---

## 7. ARCHITECTURE

Do not hardcode large website sections unnecessarily.

Prefer:

Page
→ Sections
→ Section Type
→ Section Data
→ Reusable Renderer

Use reusable components.

Examples:

- Hero
- ProjectGrid
- ProjectCard
- Gallery
- VideoSection
- TourViewer
- RoomExplorer
- ExplodedDiagram
- ProgressTimeline
- CCTVViewer
- JournalGrid
- ContactSection

Avoid creating one-off components when an existing abstraction can be extended cleanly.

---

## 8. CMS-FIRST THINKING

If the website contains an admin CMS, public content should be data-driven.

Admins should be able to control:

- Hero
- Images
- Videos
- Projects
- Galleries
- Project progress
- Posts
- Pages
- Sections
- SEO
- CTA
- Navigation
- Footer
- Construction updates

Prefer:

Database/CMS
→ API
→ Typed data
→ Section renderer
→ UI

Do not require source-code changes for normal content updates.

---

## 9. SECURITY

NEVER expose:

- API secrets
- Database credentials
- CCTV credentials
- RTSP credentials
- Private storage credentials
- Admin tokens
- Service account keys

in client-side code.

Private CCTV streams must be protected through authenticated server-side infrastructure.

Use:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Role-based access control
- Server-side validation
- Input validation
- Rate limiting
- Secure cookies
- CSRF protection where applicable
- Content Security Policy
- Secure headers
- Signed URLs for private media
- Audit logging for sensitive admin actions

Never trust client-side authorization.

---

## 10. LOADING / ERROR / EMPTY STATES

Every major experience must have:

- Loading state
- Error state
- Empty state
- Disabled state
- Offline/failure behavior where applicable

Never leave users with:

- Blank screens
- Broken images
- Infinite spinners
- Unhandled errors

---

## 11. IMAGE QUALITY

Use high-quality architectural imagery.

Images should support:

- Full-screen hero
- Project storytelling
- Detail shots
- Material textures
- Construction progress
- Interior photography
- Technical diagrams
- Before/after
- Gallery

Use appropriate cropping instead of stretching images.

Never distort images.

---

## 12. CINEMATIC EXPERIENCES

For cinematic tours:

Use a deliberate sequence.

Example:

Entrance
→ Living Room
→ Kitchen
→ Stairs
→ Hall
→ Master Bedroom
→ Bathroom

Never skip scenes.

Transitions should feel spatially connected.

---

## 13. PROJECT EXPERIENCES

A project detail page should support rich storytelling:

- Hero
- Overview
- Location
- Architecture
- Concept
- Floor plans
- Technical drawings
- Materials
- Gallery
- Video
- Construction progress
- Timeline
- Room exploration
- Interactive diagrams
- Contact CTA

Do not turn project pages into simple image grids.

---

## 14. INTERACTIVE ARCHITECTURAL EXPERIENCES

Where appropriate support:

- Exploded views
- Layer toggles
- Floor selection
- Room selection
- Material exploration
- Before/after states
- Construction stages
- Scroll-driven storytelling
- 3D/2.5D experiences
- Cinematic camera movement

Interactions must remain understandable and performant.

---

## 15. LIVE CONSTRUCTION / CCTV

For live construction monitoring:

Public users may see approved/public streams only.

Private streams require authentication and authorization.

Never expose raw:

RTSP
camera username
camera password
private camera URLs

to the browser.

Provide graceful states:

- Live
- Connecting
- Offline
- Maintenance
- Restricted

---

## 16. TYPOGRAPHY

Typography must establish hierarchy.

Define:

- Display
- H1
- H2
- H3
- Body
- Small
- Caption
- Label
- Navigation
- Metadata

Avoid excessive font weights.

Typography should feel editorial and architectural.

---

## 17. DESIGN SYSTEM

Use centralized tokens for:

- Colors
- Typography
- Spacing
- Radius
- Shadows
- Borders
- Motion
- Breakpoints
- Container widths
- Z-index
- Durations
- Easing

Do not scatter arbitrary values throughout the application.

---

## 18. MOTION SYSTEM

Define reusable motion presets.

Examples:

- Fade Up
- Fade In
- Reveal
- Scale In
- Image Zoom
- Parallax
- Slide
- Stagger
- Page Transition
- Modal Transition
- Navigation Transition

Use consistent easing and duration.

Avoid random animation values.

---

## 19. CODE QUALITY

Before implementing:

1. Inspect the existing project.
2. Inspect architecture.
3. Inspect installed dependencies.
4. Inspect existing components.
5. Inspect existing design tokens.
6. Inspect existing skills/rules.
7. Reuse what already exists.
8. Avoid unnecessary rewrites.

Do not destroy working architecture simply to implement a feature.

---

## 20. VALIDATION

After implementation:

- Run type checks.
- Run lint.
- Run tests where available.
- Check responsive layouts.
- Check console errors.
- Check broken images.
- Check animation performance.
- Check keyboard navigation.
- Check reduced motion.
- Check loading/error states.
- Check authentication boundaries.
- Check mobile behavior.

Do not consider a feature complete until it works visually and technically.

---

## FINAL PRINCIPLE

Build the website as if it will be reviewed by:

- A world-class architect
- A premium design studio
- A senior frontend engineer
- A motion designer
- A security engineer
- A performance engineer

The final result must feel intentional, cinematic, technically excellent, responsive, accessible, secure, and production-ready.
